\subsubsection{Sensitivity and degradation cases}
\label{sec:ou-robustness}
The robustness study is retained as a sensitivity analysis of the deployed
OU--III architecture rather than as a comparison among alternative adaptation
laws.  At the nominal $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ operating point, one-factor-at-a-time
(OFAT) sweeps perturb $\tau$, $\sigma_{aw}$, or $r_S$ by multipliers $0.5$,
$0.75$, $1.0$, $1.25$, and $1.5$ while holding the other two quantities fixed.
These three sweeps isolate the direct process-covariance and integral-state
regularization sensitivities and are independent of how an online adaptation
law couples the parameters.

Two additional coupled sweeps evaluate the same perturbations along the
\emph{deployed SpectralMSE manifold}.  With the physical acceleration scale
perturbed by a factor $c$ at fixed $\tau$ and cadence,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{r_S(c\sigma_a,\tau)}{r_S(\sigma_a,\tau)}=c^{6/7}.
 \label{eq:robustness-sigma-coupling}
\end{equation}
For a correlation-time perturbation $\tau\mapsto c\tau$ at fixed acceleration
scale,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{r_S(c\tau)}{r_S(\tau)}
 =c^{24/7}\sqrt{\frac{T_S(\tau)}{T_S(c\tau)}}.
 \label{eq:robustness-tau-coupling}
\end{equation}
Away from the pseudo-update cadence clamps, $T_S\propto\tau$ and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:robustness-tau-coupling} reduces to
$r_S\mapsto c^{41/14}r_S$.  The study therefore keeps the useful coupled
sensitivity experiment while replacing the retired dimensional cubic coupling
with the analytical law actually used by the filter.

The committed pre-SpectralMSE robustness archive remains available for
provenance, but its old coupled columns are not reused as current numerical
evidence.  The publication input below always retains the law-independent OFAT
results; the two coupled columns and sensitivity figure are admitted only when
they have been regenerated by the current SpectralMSE robustness driver.

\IfFileExists{w3d-ou-robustness-results-generated.tex-part}{%
  \input{w3d-ou-robustness-results-generated.tex-part}%
}{}

\IfFileExists{ou_robustness_sensitivity.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_robustness_sensitivity}
    \caption{OU--III sensitivity about the nominal operating point under the
    deployed SpectralMSE regularizer.  Solid curves are OFAT perturbations;
    dashed curves follow the analytical coupling in
    Eqs.~\eqref{eq:robustness-sigma-coupling}--\eqref{eq:robustness-tau-coupling}.
    Error bars are paired-bootstrap 95\% intervals and the vertical marker is
    the nominal point.}
    \label{fig:ou_robustness_sensitivity}
  \end{figure}
}{}

The OFAT sweep remains informative even before the coupled rows are regenerated.
Across the declared multiplier range, varying $\tau$ alone changes mean
normalized vertical error by \OURobustnessTauSpan{} percentage points of $H_s$,
changing $\sigma_{aw}$ alone changes it by \OURobustnessSigmaSpan{} points, and
changing $r_S$ alone changes it by \OURobustnessRSSpan{} points.  Thus the
latent-acceleration prior is comparatively insensitive over this local range,
whereas the unobserved low-frequency integral drift remains more responsive to
the pseudo-measurement strength.  This does not make $\tau$ or $\sigma_{aw}$
unused: the accelerometer observes the latent acceleration directly at
\SI{200}{Hz}, so moderate prior rescaling has less effect on vertical
displacement once that channel is information-rich.  Attitude can remain more
sensitive because the latent-acceleration covariance also enters the
accelerometer innovation covariance.

Two ten-seed degradation cases extend the main validation envelope without
changing the deployed law.  The first uses a low-motion $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$
record to expose the approximately fixed absolute sensor-error floor.  The
second compresses the controlled transition between the same endpoints from
\SI{120}{s} to \SI{30}{s}; Adaptive and FixedNominal are both scored so the
transition degradation remains separated from the fixed operating-point
baseline.

\IfFileExists{ou_robustness_stress.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_robustness_stress}
    \caption{Low-motion and rapid-transition degradation cases.}
    \label{fig:ou_robustness_stress}
  \end{figure}
}{}

At $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$, the mean absolute vertical RMS error is
\SI{\OURobustnessLowStressAbsolute}{m}, corresponding to
\OURobustnessLowStressMean\% of $H_s$; the increase in normalized error exposes
the approximately fixed absolute sensor-error floor.  Compressing the
transition from \SI{120}{s} to \SI{30}{s} changes the Adaptive mean from
\OURobustnessControlledAdaptiveMean\% to
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptiveMean\% of the final $H_s$.  This rapid-case difference
is a degradation result rather than a pure adaptation-rate measurement because
compressing the crossfade also changes the composition of the fixed
\SI{900}{s} scoring window.  Isolating adaptation bandwidth would require a
protocol that holds the scored sea-state composition fixed while varying only
the transition rate.
